There are several ways to make an HTTP request in JavaScript, depending on the browser and the JavaScript library or framework you're using. Here are a few common methods:

**Using the `XMLHttpRequest` object (older browsers):**

```
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://example.com/api/data', true);
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(xhr.responseText);
  }
};
xhr.send();
```

**Using the `fetch` API (modern browsers):**

```
fetch('https://example.com/api/data')
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```

**Using jQuery (if you're using the jQuery library):**

```
$.ajax({
  type: 'GET',
  url: 'https://example.com/api/data',
  dataType: 'text',
  success: function(data) {
    console.log(data);
  }
});
```

In each of these examples, we're making a GET request to the URL `https://example.com/api/data`. You can modify the URL, method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.), and other parameters to suit your needs.

